\begin{abstract}
Autonomous web agents powered by Large Language Models (LLMs) are widely evaluated on long-horizon web navigation and digital commerce tasks. State-of-the-art benchmarks (e.g., WebArena, Mind2Web, WorkArena) evaluate agents holistically at the whole-task level, treating web pages as monolithic HTML documents, accessibility hierarchies, or rendered screenshot streams. Consequently, existing literature leaves unexamined the client-side Document Object Model (DOM) observation pipelines that filter, prune, and transform web markup before it enters the LLM context prompt. In modern web engineering, factual information is distributed across distinct technical layers: rendered typography, semantic accessibility annotations (WAI-ARIA), developer custom attributes (\texttt{data-*}), and structured metadata (Schema.org JSON-LD).

In this paper, we present an empirical systems audit of the \textbf{DOM serialization gap}---the structural discrepancy between what exists in the client-side DOM and what actually reaches the LLM context prompt. Through source-code analysis and dynamic observation tracing of modern browser-agent infrastructure (\texttt{browser-use 0.13.10}), we document three critical architectural bottlenecks:
(1) default DOM serializers unconditionally prune \texttt{<script>} and \texttt{<meta>} elements via hardcoded exclusion rules (\texttt{DISABLED\_ELEMENTS}), preventing Schema.org JSON-LD structured data from reaching the LLM prompt buffer ($0.0\%$ token reachability);
(2) the serializer constructs HTML element tags and attributes \textbf{only for interactive (clickable/scrollable) elements or frames} (\texttt{serializer.py:L1046--L1058}), meaning non-interactive elements (\texttt{<span>}, \texttt{<div>}, \texttt{<section>}) have their tags stripped entirely and parent attributes discarded ($0.0\%$ attribute reachability); and
(3) custom developer attributes (\texttt{data-*}) are stripped by default whitelist filtering unless explicitly overridden by custom runner configuration.

Through direct Chromium Chrome DevTools Protocol (CDP) accessibility tree extraction (\texttt{Accessibility.getFullAXTree}), we refute the widespread assumption that ``DOM pruning is equivalent to an accessibility tree.'' We document a \textbf{Critical Divergence}: non-interactive ARIA labels are retained by Chromium Blink's native accessibility tree but $100\%$ stripped by agent DOM serializers; conversely, developer \texttt{data-*} attributes are absent from accessibility trees but can be delivered to agents under extended whitelisting. By pairing Blink's unique \texttt{backendDOMNodeId}, we establish a 1-to-1 node-level correspondence rate of $79.4\%$ (27 of 34 interactive elements) on route \texttt{\#home}, identifying shadow DOM boundaries as the primary source of alignment divergence.

Furthermore, we expose the \textbf{Action-Label Co-Location Confound}: augmenting button labels with target product titles converts hierarchical reference resolution into trivial string matching. We pre-register a hardened 4-arm evaluation blueprint equipped with deterministic state-asserted verifiers and zero-dependency statistical estimators (Cochran--Mantel--Haenszel, Newcombe hybrid score CIs). Monte Carlo power simulations (10,000 trials) prove that small-sample agent experiments ($n=20$/arm) possess $<11\%$ power to detect realistic effect sizes ($\Delta \le 0.20$), establishing a strict methodological prohibition against the Equivalence Fallacy. Finally, we report a diagnostic case study on local quantized models (\texttt{qwen3:4b}), demonstrating how cumulative context expansion causes prompt evaluation latency to breach fixed 180-second step watchdogs, and proving how cancellation telemetry safely captures in-flight timeouts without corrupting server metrics.
\end{abstract}

\textbf{Keywords:} Autonomous Web Agents, Large Language Models, DOM Serialization Gap, WAI-ARIA, Semantic Markup, Accessibility Trees, Experimental Reproducibility.

\section{Introduction and Research Motivation}
\label{sec:intro}

The integration of Large Language Models (LLMs) with automated browser execution environments has catalyzed the emergence of autonomous web agents capable of executing multi-turn transactional workflows across digital interfaces~\cite{zhou2024webarena, deng2023mind2web, drouin2024workarena}. Operating as sequential decision-making agents, these systems observe the state of a web page, synthesize an internal plan, and dispatch discrete actions (e.g., clicking elements, entering text, selecting dropdown options, or submitting forms) via browser automation protocols such as Playwright, Puppeteer, or the Chrome DevTools Protocol (CDP).

Despite widespread empirical benchmarking, contemporary web agent research suffers from a pervasive methodological confound: evaluations are conducted holistically across diverse, heterogeneous web environments where visual design, page geometry, JavaScript routing logic, and DOM markup representations vary simultaneously. On complex websites, it is impossible to disentangle whether an agent failed due to visual layout occlusion, long context saturation, stochastic planning drift, or uninformative DOM node labeling.

In modern frontend web engineering, information is intentionally partitioned into distinct architectural layers:
\begin{itemize}
    \item \textbf{Rendered Visible Typography:} Plain textual nodes rendered inside standard HTML tags (e.g., \texttt{<h1>}, \texttt{<p>}, \texttt{<span>}) designed for human visual reading.
    \item \textbf{Semantic Accessibility Markup (WAI-ARIA):} Programmatic semantic annotations (e.g., \texttt{role}, \texttt{aria-label}, \texttt{aria-describedby}) standardized by the World Wide Web Consortium~\cite{wai_aria11} to convey interactive semantics to assistive screen readers.
    \item \textbf{Developer Custom Data Attributes:} Structured key-value pairs (e.g., \texttt{data-action}, \texttt{data-product-id}, \texttt{data-category}) utilized by client-side Single Page Applications (SPAs) for state management.
    \item \textbf{Structured Metadata (Schema.org JSON-LD):} Standardized machine-readable entity graphs embedded inside non-rendered script tags (\texttt{<script type="application/ld+json">}) for search engine indexing~\cite{schemaorg}.
\end{itemize}

As industry discussions propose modifying web architectures for autonomous agents---including proposals to enrich ARIA labels specifically for AI scrapers or to deploy dedicated machine endpoints (e.g., \texttt{llms.txt}, Model Context Protocol APIs)---a fundamental systems question arises:
\begin{quote}
\textit{Do client-side DOM serializers in modern autonomous agent frameworks preserve these metadata channels, or do heuristic pruning algorithms strip them before they can reach the LLM context prompt?}
\end{quote}

If an agent framework silently purges structured metadata, web developers' efforts to markup pages for AI agents are completely lost. Furthermore, if academic researchers attempt to benchmark agent performance across markup variants without verifying prompt delivery, they risk evaluating an unaugmented text baseline while mistakenly attributing performance variations to invisible channels.

\section{Related Work and Systems Gap}
\label{sec:related}

\subsection{Autonomous Web Agent Benchmarks}
Evaluation of LLMs as autonomous web agents has advanced through several major benchmark suites:
\begin{itemize}
    \item \textbf{WebArena}~\cite{zhou2024webarena} established an end-to-end evaluation paradigm across four realistic self-hosted web applications. Evaluating state-of-the-art LLMs (GPT-4), WebArena revealed an end-to-end task completion rate of only $10.6\%$--$14.4\%$. However, WebArena evaluates holistic task success across multi-page workflows, conflating layout design, visual rendering, and markup structure.
    \item \textbf{Mind2Web}~\cite{deng2023mind2web} introduced a large-scale dataset of 2,350 tasks across 137 real websites, evaluating element-level action prediction offline from DOM tree snapshots. While establishing the severity of the HTML token context bottleneck, Mind2Web evaluates static snapshots rather than closed-loop dynamic navigation.
    \item \textbf{WorkArena and BrowserGym}~\cite{drouin2024workarena} evaluated web agents on enterprise workflows within ServiceNow. To mitigate context window exhaustion, BrowserGym utilizes Accessibility Trees (AXTrees), condensing 15,000+ token raw HTML documents into 200--400 tokens of interactive element representations. BrowserGym demonstrated the operational token efficiency of accessibility representations, but treated AXTree generation as an operational shortcut rather than auditing whether semantic attributes inside the tree provide additive cognitive utility.
    \item \textbf{WebAgent and HTML-T5}~\cite{gur2024webagent} addressed the HTML length bottleneck by pre-training a specialized model (HTML-T5) to sub-sample and filter long HTML documents prior to action planning, proving that preprocessing the DOM directly bounds downstream agent execution.
    \item \textbf{AgentBench}~\cite{liu2024agentbench} evaluated LLMs across eight interactive environments, confirming that long-horizon web decision-making exhibits substantial performance drops relative to static NLP reasoning.
\end{itemize}

\subsection{Multimodal Visual Grounding vs. Text Observation}
A parallel research line integrates computer vision with web navigation:
\begin{itemize}
    \item \textbf{VisualWebArena}~\cite{koh2024visualwebarena} extended WebArena to visually intensive tasks, demonstrating that text-only agents fail when critical information is encoded exclusively in pixels.
    \item \textbf{SeeAct}~\cite{zheng2024seeact} formulated a two-stage visual agent framework separating candidate generation on screenshots from action grounding on DOM elements, demonstrating that visual perception assists planning but suffers from fine-grained action grounding bottlenecks.
    \item \textbf{WebVoyager}~\cite{he2024webvoyager} evaluated multimodal agents using Set-of-Mark visual prompts across 15 live websites, achieving $55.7\%$ completion. However, visual screenshot prompts bypass the DOM observation pipeline entirely, precluding the study of structural markup channels.
\end{itemize}

\subsection{The Unaddressed Systems Gap}
Prior literature addresses task complexity, visual grounding, and model capabilities. \textbf{However, no prior study has systematically audited the client-side DOM serialization algorithms in open-source agent libraries to measure information loss between source HTML, live DOM trees, browser accessibility trees, and final LLM prompt buffers.}

\section{Systems Architecture and Pruning Heuristics}
\label{sec:architecture}

We performed a static AST source-code audit and runtime trace of \texttt{browser-use 0.13.10}, a widely deployed open-source browser agent framework.

\subsection{Script and Meta Pruning (\texttt{DISABLED\_ELEMENTS})}
In \texttt{browser\_use/dom/serializer/serializer.py} (Line 18), the framework defines an explicit blacklist:
\begin{lstlisting}[language=Python]
DISABLED_ELEMENTS = {'style', 'script', 'head', 'meta', 'link', 'title'}
\end{lstlisting}
During simplified tree creation (\texttt{\_create\_simplified\_tree}, Lines 480--482):
\begin{lstlisting}[language=Python]
elif node.node_type == NodeType.ELEMENT_NODE:
    if node.node_name.lower() in DISABLED_ELEMENTS:
        return None
\end{lstlisting}
Any element matching \texttt{DISABLED\_ELEMENTS} is immediately pruned from the observation graph; child nodes are never traversed. Consequently, \texttt{<script type="application/ld+json">} structured data and \texttt{<meta name="evidence-date">} tags suffer complete, irreversible $100\%$ information loss.

\subsection{Non-Interactive Element Tag Stripping}
In \texttt{browser\_use/dom/serializer/serializer.py} (Lines 1046--1058), element tag serialization is conditionally gated:
\begin{lstlisting}[language=Python]
is_any_scrollable = node.original_node.is_actually_scrollable or node.original_node.is_scrollable
if (
    node.is_interactive
    or is_any_scrollable
    or node.original_node.tag_name.upper() == 'IFRAME'
    or node.original_node.tag_name.upper() == 'FRAME'
):
    next_depth += 1
    attributes_html_str = DOMTreeSerializer._build_attributes_string(
        node.original_node, include_attributes, text_content
    )
\end{lstlisting}
If an element is \textbf{not interactive} (not clickable, not scrollable, and not an iframe):
\begin{itemize}
    \item \texttt{\_build\_attributes\_string} is never executed.
    \item No opening or closing tag is output for that element.
    \item The serializer processes child text nodes, emitting raw strings, but all element attributes (\texttt{aria-label}, \texttt{data-*}, \texttt{role}) are discarded.
\end{itemize}

\subsection{Attribute Whitelist Filtering}
Attribute retention on interactive elements is governed by \texttt{DEFAULT\_INCLUDE\_ATTRIBUTES} (\texttt{browser\_use/dom/views.py}, Line 18):
\begin{lstlisting}[language=Python]
DEFAULT_INCLUDE_ATTRIBUTES = [
    'title', 'type', 'checked', 'id', 'name', 'role', 
    'value', 'placeholder', 'alt', 'aria-label', ...
]
\end{lstlisting}
While standard accessibility attributes (\texttt{aria-label}, \texttt{role}) are included, developer custom attributes (\texttt{data-action}, \texttt{data-product-id}) are omitted. In \texttt{serializer.py} (Lines 1211--1215), attributes are discarded if \texttt{key not in include\_attributes}.

\section{Empirical Systems Audit: The 5-Stage Observation Pipeline}
\label{sec:audit}

\subsection{The 5 Observation Pipeline Stages}
We define five discrete architectural stages through which web information flows before model ingestion:
\begin{enumerate}
    \item \textbf{Stage 1 (Source HTML \& Static Markup):} Raw HTML markup received over HTTP.
    \item \textbf{Stage 2 (Browser Live DOM):} In-memory DOM tree parsed by Blink and augmented by client-side JavaScript.
    \item \textbf{Stage 3 (Browser Accessibility Tree):} Chromium Blink native \texttt{AXObjectCache} queried via CDP \texttt{Accessibility.getFullAXTree}.
    \item \textbf{Stage 4 (Agent DOM Serializer):} The simplified tree constructed by \texttt{browser-use 0.13.10}.
    \item \textbf{Stage 5 (Final Model Context Prompt):} The stringified observation buffer compiled into the LLM context.
\end{enumerate}

