\subsection{Node-Level Correspondence: Chromium AXTree vs. Selector Map}
To evaluate structural alignment between Chromium's native accessibility tree and the agent's observation tree, we implemented \texttt{compare\_accessibility\_tree.py}:
\begin{itemize}
    \item CDP \texttt{Accessibility.getFullAXTree} extracts Blink's unique \texttt{backendDOMNodeId} for every accessibility node.
    \item In \texttt{browser-use 0.13.10}, \texttt{summary.dom\_state.selector\_map} indexes interactive elements containing the matching Blink \texttt{backend\_node\_id}.
\end{itemize}

\textbf{Empirical Findings on Route \texttt{\#home}:}
Across 336 Chromium AXNodes constructed across variants (256 possessing \texttt{backendDOMNodeId}), \texttt{browser-use} detected 34 interactive elements. Exactly \textbf{27 of 34 interactive elements ($79.4\%$)} matched 1-to-1 to a corresponding Blink AXNode. The 7 unmatched elements corresponded to shadow DOM boundaries and synthetic input wrappers where Blink does not generate top-level accessibility nodes matching the DOM backend ID.

\subsection{Refutation of the ``DOM Pruning == Accessibility Tree'' Fallacy}
Our multi-layer extraction disproves the common assumption that agent DOM pruning approximates an accessibility tree:
\begin{itemize}
    \item \textbf{Critical Divergence (Non-Interactive ARIA):} Retained by Blink's AXTree (e.g., accessible name \texttt{"Price: \$49.99"}), but \textbf{$100\%$ stripped} by \texttt{browser-use}. Screen readers receive structured semantics, but LLM agents receive bare typography.
    \item \textbf{Reversed Divergence (Custom \texttt{data-*}):} Absent from Blink's AXTree, but \textbf{$100\%$ deliverable} to agents on interactive elements under an extended whitelist.
\end{itemize}

\section{Methodological Analysis and Experimental Confounds}
\label{sec:methodology}

\subsection{The Action-Label Co-Location Confound}
When ARIA markup does reach the prompt (on \texttt{<button>} elements), modifying button labels from generic text (e.g., \texttt{"Add to Cart"}) to entity-specific ARIA labels (e.g., \texttt{aria-label="Add SoundFlow Air 3 to cart"}) introduces an acute experimental confound:
\begin{itemize}
    \item In unaugmented baseline (\textsc{ABS}): The agent observes:
    \begin{quote}
    \texttt{SoundFlow Air 3} \\
    \texttt{\$49.99} \\
    \texttt{[12] <button>Add to Cart</button>}
    \end{quote}
    The agent must perform \textbf{hierarchical reference resolution}: locate the card boundary, verify product title and price, and infer that button \texttt{[12]} belongs to SoundFlow Air 3.
    \item In confounded ARIA: The agent observes:
    \begin{quote}
    \texttt{[12] <button aria-label="Add SoundFlow Air 3 to cart">Add to Cart</button>}
    \end{quote}
    The agent performs \textbf{direct string matching}: finding \texttt{"SoundFlow Air 3"} directly within the button tag. Any observed accuracy improvement measures label disambiguation, not semantic markup comprehension.
\end{itemize}
To eliminate this confound, our de-confounded stimulus design in \texttt{shared/app-core.js:L35} standardizes generic action triggers across all variants: \texttt{addBtn.setAttribute('aria-label', 'Add item to cart')}.

\subsection{Hardened State-Asserted Verifiers}
To prevent scoring inflation, all verifiers enforce deterministic DOM state assertions:
\begin{itemize}
    \item \textbf{T1--T2:} Programmatic assertion of exact SKU matches in \texttt{window.\_\_cart\_\_}.
    \item \textbf{T3 (2nd-Highest Audio):} Strictly enforces \texttt{len(cart) == 1 and cart[0] == "wh-002"}, rejecting the 6-SKU pass vulnerability of earlier verifiers.
    \item \textbf{T4 (Transactional Checkout):} Enforces \texttt{order\_confirmed == True} via DOM form submission assertion, eliminating partial credit on incomplete checkouts.
    \item \textbf{T5 (Cart Removal):} Asserts precondition \texttt{pre\_cart\_count == 1} before checking empty final cart (\texttt{len(cart) == 0}).
    \item \textbf{T6 (Absent Fact Probe):} Evaluates grounded refusal vs. hallucinated warranty duration in strict isolation from primary task accuracy.
\end{itemize}
All verifiers pass $100\%$ of offline regression tests (\texttt{test\_t6\_verifier\_isolated.py}, \texttt{test\_verifier\_ground\_truth\_battery.py}).

\section{Statistical Power Boundaries and The Equivalence Fallacy}
\label{sec:power}

\subsection{Task-Stratified Cochran--Mantel--Haenszel (CMH) and Newcombe CIs}
To account for task heterogeneity across $K=5$ primary strata, post-experiment inference utilizes the continuity-corrected CMH test statistic~\cite{agresti2002categorical}:
\begin{equation}
\chi^2_{\text{CMH}} = \frac{\left( \left| \sum_{k=1}^K n_{11k} - \sum_{k=1}^K \mu_k \right| - 0.5 \right)^2}{\sum_{k=1}^K \text{Var}(n_{11k})}
\end{equation}
Risk differences are reported alongside 95\% Newcombe hybrid score confidence intervals~\cite{newcombe1998interval} utilizing independent Wilson score limits~\cite{wilson1927probable}. In \texttt{test\_analyze\_v3\_results.py}, our zero-dependency Python implementation matches published clinical trial benchmarks to four decimal places (Agresti 2002: $\chi^2 = 6.3841, p = 0.0115$; Newcombe 1998: $\hat{\theta} = 0.3250$, 95\% CI: $[+0.1764, +0.4578]$).

\subsection{Monte Carlo Power Analysis}
Primary contrasts across tasks T1--T5 utilize $n=20$ trials per arm. 10,000-trial Monte Carlo simulations (\texttt{power\_analysis.json}) establish:
\begin{itemize}
    \item $\Delta = 0.70$: $100.0\%$ power (robustly detectable).
    \item $\Delta = 0.35$: $49.7\% \pm 1.1\%$ power.
    \item $\Delta = 0.20$: $\mathbf{10.8\% \pm 0.6\%}$ power (Bonferroni $\alpha = 0.0167$).
\end{itemize}

\textbf{Methodological Prohibition of Equivalence Claims:}
Because the power to detect small performance differences ($\Delta \le 0.20$) is under $11\%$, obtaining $p > 0.05$ cannot be interpreted as evidence that plain HTML and semantic markup perform equivalently. A null result indicates only that the sample size was insufficient to detect subtle effects.

