\subsection{Context Expansion and Latency Scaling}
In Step 1 of \texttt{qwen3\_smoke\_003}, the prompt was $\sim$1,500 tokens. On hybrid CPU/GPU compute (34\% CPU / 66\% GPU on 4 GB VRAM), \texttt{qwen3:4b} completed prompt evaluation and action generation in 102.9 seconds. In Step 2, \texttt{browser-use} appended the prior step history, action result, and updated DOM, expanding context to $>3,200$ tokens. At hybrid ingestion rates ($\sim$15--20 tokens/sec), processing 3,200 tokens required $>180$ seconds, triggering the outer step watchdog ceiling.

\subsection{In-Flight Cancellation Telemetry}
In \texttt{browser-use}, step timeouts are enforced via:
\begin{lstlisting}[language=Python]
await asyncio.wait_for(self.step(step_info), timeout=self.settings.step_timeout)
\end{lstlisting}
When the 180-second timeout expires, \texttt{asyncio.wait\_for} injects \texttt{asyncio.CancelledError}. In Python 3.8+, \texttt{CancelledError} inherits directly from \texttt{BaseException}, not \texttt{Exception}. In run 004, the wrapper's \texttt{except Exception:} handler was bypassed, resulting in empty telemetry (\texttt{model\_call\_telemetry: []}). 

In run 005, implementing dedicated \texttt{except asyncio.CancelledError:} safely captured:
\begin{itemize}
    \item \texttt{call\_index}: 1, \texttt{status}: \texttt{"cancelled\_in\_flight"}, \texttt{error\_type}: \texttt{"CancelledError"}.
    \item \texttt{wall\_clock\_seconds}: 179.6983s.
    \item Unobserved server metrics (\texttt{prompt\_tokens}, \texttt{eval\_duration\_ms}) were strictly preserved as \texttt{null}.
\end{itemize}

\section{Discussion and Implications for Web Standards}
\label{sec:discussion}

\subsection{The Fallacy of Metadata Augmentation for AI}
Web authors cannot assume that adding Schema.org JSON-LD or custom data attributes will assist AI agents. Under standard \texttt{browser-use}, client-side DOM serializers discard these elements before the LLM receives the observation.

\subsection{The ``ARIA Stuffing'' Hazard}
Standards bodies (W3C/TPAC) have warned against developers overloading ARIA attributes specifically to optimize for AI agents. If ARIA attributes provide no generalized accuracy advantage over clear typography---and merely duplicate entity text onto buttons---stuffing ARIA tags risks polluting accessibility trees for human screen-reader users without aiding AI agents.

\subsection{The Necessity of Out-of-Band Machine Endpoints}
Rather than attempting to embed machine data in HTML DOM channels that are vulnerable to serialization pruning, standards organizations should prioritize dedicated machine endpoints (e.g., \texttt{llms.txt}, Model Context Protocol APIs) that bypass DOM serialization entirely.

\section{Conclusion}
\label{sec:conclusion}

This paper formalizes the DOM serialization gap in autonomous browser agents, establishing that default observation pipelines discard rich structured metadata (Schema.org JSON-LD and developer custom attributes) prior to model prompt construction. We have empirically documented the Critical Divergence between browser accessibility trees and DOM serializers, identified the action-label co-location confound, and proven the statistical power boundaries of small-sample web agent experiments. By providing a 100\% reproducible offline verification suite, this work establishes essential systems baselines for future web agent engineering.

